@meta
id: 08-13
title: Copy a line and edit the copy
chapter: 08
type: lesson
skills: yank-y, put-p, change-c, replace-r
par: 40
minutes: 5
vscode: select the line, Ctrl-C, Ctrl-V, then fix the copy
open: default.json

@brief
Delete puts things in the register. So does copy, and in vim copy is
called *yank* — `y` — because `c` was already taken by change.

Yank behaves exactly like delete except that the text stays where it is.
`yy` takes a line, `p` puts it back. Together they are the fastest way to
write a line that is nearly the same as one already in the file, and the
error registry is full of those.

Add `ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED` to the registry, directly under
`ORDER_NOT_FOUND`. It is a client error, so `sentry` stays `false`, and
its status code is 409.

Copy the line you already have. Do not type the whole thing.

@keys
| `yy` | yank the current line into the register |
| `p` | put it back below |
| `cw` | change to the end of the word |
| `r` | replace one character |
| `f4` `;` | forward to a `4`, then the next one |

@hints
The line you need is almost identical to the one above it. Make a copy
first and correct the copy afterwards.
--
`yy` then `p` gives you a duplicate on the line below, with the cursor on
it. From there only the code name and one digit are wrong.
--
`j yy p` makes the copy. `^ f_ l cw` then `ALREADY_SHIPPED` and `Esc`
fixes the name. `f4 ; r9` turns 404 into 409.

@solution
    j yy p  ^f_l cwALREADY_SHIPPED<Esc>  f4;r9  :wq<Enter>

Notice how little of this was typing and how much of it was aiming. That
is the trade vim keeps offering: a few precise keys instead of a lot of
retyping, and the parts of the line you never touched cannot be wrong.

`yy` is worth its own habit even when you are not about to paste. Yank a
line before you rewrite it and you have a copy in the register for free,
which is a second undo that survives the first one.

Vim has a capital `Y` as well. Sensibly it would mean "yank to the end of
the line", to match `D` and `C` in the next lesson but one. It does not —
`Y` is just another `yy`. It is the most complained-about key in vim, and
a lot of people remap it. Know the trap and move on.

@start default.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "PAYMENT_REFUND_FAILED": { "statusCode": 502, "sentry": true }
}

@expected default.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false },
  "PAYMENT_REFUND_FAILED": { "statusCode": 502, "sentry": true }
}
